    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Gem3P Timmy has Visions of Beyond available with {U} from untapped Watery Grave, and the opponent's graveyard has 19 cards — just one short of the 20-card threshold for drawing three cards instead of one. However, even drawing one card for {U} at end of opponent's turn would be reasonable value. More importantly, the opponent's graveyard is at 19 cards and will very likely hit 20 soon (Ruin Crab triggers, etc.), so waiting a turn to cast Visions could yield three cards. That said, passing on both spells when there's nothing else to do with mana is leaving value on the table. The opponent has no creatures to Fatal Push, so that's fine to hold. But Visions of Beyond could have been cast at end of opponent's next turn when the graveyard likely hits 20. Actually, looking more carefully: Gem3P passed their own main phase — they can still cast at end of opponent's turn. Wait, the choices say 'Play instants and activated abilities' during POSTCOMBAT_MAIN. They chose False (passed). They could cast Visions now for 1 card, or wait for opponent's end step. Since the opponent's graveyard is at 19 cards, and Ruin Crab will trigger on Gem3P's next land drop potentially milling 3 more cards to push it over 20, waiting until next turn to cast Visions for 3 cards is likely correct. Passing here is actually reasonable to maximize Visions of Beyond value. However, they also have Oboro, Palace in the Clouds in hand which they didn't play this turn — that's a land drop that would trigger Ruin Crab to mill 3, pushing the opponent's graveyard to 22 cards, and THEN they could cast Visions of Beyond for 3 cards. This is the real miss: play Oboro (triggers Ruin Crab, mills 3, opponent GY goes to 22), then cast Visions of Beyond drawing 3 cards.",
